# The corpus is never committed. See README, section "The corpus".
#
# Two rules, because one was not enough. data/* is the tidy case. *.pdf is the
# real one: the first time a corpus was put on a machine by hand it landed at
# the repository root, where data/* says nothing about it, and `git add -A`
# would have committed it. A rule that protects a location only protects the
# people who use that location.
data/*
!data/.gitkeep
*.pdf

# Vector stores are derived artifacts. Rebuild them with ingest.py.
#
# By shape, not by name. chroma_db/ was the name the default produces, but
# RAG_CHROMA_PATH makes the path a setting, and a second corpus needs a second
# store: chroma_obliqa/ showed up untracked the first time ObliQA was ingested.
# Same defect as IA-139 for the third time, four lines under the comment above
# that says a rule protecting a location only protects the people who use that
# location. An absolute path in the environment still escapes this. An ignore
# file cannot chase a variable; it can cover every name a person would pick.
chroma*/

# Evaluation runs are reproducible output, except the ones promoted to
# eval/results/ on purpose as a recorded baseline.
eval/*.json
!eval/results/*.json

# Real token counts and real money. Local, never committed.
eval/usage-log.jsonl

venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.DS_Store
